Browsers enforce a **same-origin policy**: by default, JavaScript on a page served from https://app.example.com cannot read the response to a fetch/XHR request it makes to a different origin, like https://api.othercompany.com, even though the request can often still be *sent*. The browser blocks the *page's own script* from reading the response, which protects against a malicious page silently pulling data from a service the user is logged into elsewhere. **CORS** (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) is how a server opts back in, allowing specific other origins to read its responses, via response headers like Access-Control-Allow-Origin. For requests that could have side effects (a JSON POST, a custom header, a PUT or DELETE), the browser first sends a 'preflight' OPTIONS request asking whether the real request is allowed, and only sends the real one if the answer is yes; the cors middleware answers preflights for you.

A detail that trips people up: CORS is a **browser-enforced** restriction on browser-based JavaScript. It does nothing to stop a server calling another server (via curl, another Node process's fetch, a mobile app's network layer), because there's no browser or same-origin policy involved. A missing or misconfigured CORS header only breaks or protects browser-based cross-origin calls. It isn't an access control mechanism for your API as a whole and should never be your only defense against unauthorized access; that's what authentication and authorization are for.

A common shortcut, when a CORS error is blocking development and someone wants it to go away, is setting Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * (allow any origin) instead of configuring the set of trusted origins that need access:

```javascript
const cors = require('cors');

// Risky for anything that isn't meant to be public: any website can now read
// this API's (non-credentialed) responses from a visitor's browser
app.use(cors());   // defaults to Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *

// Better: only the specific origins that should actually be allowed
app.use(cors({
  origin: ['https://app.example.com', 'https://admin.example.com'],
}));
```

With the wildcard, any website's JavaScript can read this API's responses to requests that don't carry credentials. That's harmless for a deliberately public API, but a problem when the response depends on where the visitor's browser sits (an internal service, a service on localhost, an IP-restricted API) or wasn't meant to be readable by arbitrary sites. For cookie-based auth the rule is stricter: Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * cannot be combined with Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true, and browsers reject a credentialed response that uses the wildcard. Developers who hit that error often 'fix' it by echoing back whatever Origin the request sent (cors({ origin: true, credentials: true })), and that configuration is the dangerous one: it lets any site make credentialed requests as the logged-in user and read the answers, exactly the cross-origin data theft the same-origin policy exists to prevent. So the safe pattern is an explicit allowlist of trusted origins.

Rate limiting caps how many requests a client (typically identified by IP address, API key, or authenticated user ID) can make within a time window, and it protects against several problems at once. It defends against a **denial-of-service** attempt, where an attacker floods the server to exhaust capacity and deny service to legitimate users. It defends against **brute-force** attacks on something like a login endpoint, where an attacker (or a buggy client) tries many password guesses in rapid succession; limiting the login route makes that impractically slow, though not impossible. And it defends against **runaway cost**, which matters for any API that calls a metered, pay-per-request downstream service (an SMS provider, an LLM API, cloud storage): a client, malicious or stuck in a retry loop, hammering your API can become a large, unexpected bill from whatever paid service you call on each request.

A fixed-window limiter keeps a counter per client key, increments it on each request, and resets it when the time window elapses:

```javascript
const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');

const limiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000,   // 15-minute window
  max: 100,                    // 100 requests per window per client
  standardHeaders: true,       // sends RateLimit-* headers so clients can see their own limits
  message: { error: 'Too many requests, please try again later' },
});

app.use('/api/', limiter);

// A stricter limiter specifically for a sensitive, brute-forceable route
const loginLimiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000,
  max: 5,                       // only 5 login attempts per 15 minutes per client
});

app.post('/login', loginLimiter, loginHandler);
```

A fixed window has a known, minor edge case: a client could send its full quota at the very end of one window and its full quota again at the very start of the next, getting close to double the intended rate over a short straddling interval. A token-bucket or sliding-window algorithm smooths this out, at the cost of more bookkeeping. For most APIs a fixed window is an adequate, simple default, and a more sophisticated algorithm is an optimization for when the simple version proves insufficient. Two practical points: the counters live in the process's memory by default, so with several server instances each one counts separately unless you configure a shared store such as Redis; and behind a reverse proxy or load balancer, req.ip is the proxy's address unless you tell Express to trust the proxy (app.set('trust proxy', ...)), in which case every client shares one limit.

examples.code:
const express = require('express');
const helmet = require('helmet');
const app = express();

app.use(helmet());

app.get('/health', (req, res) => res.json({ status: 'ok' }));

app.listen(3000);

// A response to GET /health now includes headers such as:
// X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
// X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
// Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=<seconds>; includeSubDomains   (default max-age varies by helmet version)
// (plus several others helmet sets by default)

const cors = require('cors');

// Risky for anything that isn't meant to be public: ANY origin, including a malicious
// page in another browser tab, can read this API's (non-credentialed) responses
// fragment: assumes `app` exists
app.use(cors());

// Better: an explicit allowlist of the actual trusted frontend origins
const allowedOrigins = ['https://app.example.com', 'https://admin.example.com'];

app.use(cors({
  origin: (origin, callback) => {
    if (!origin || allowedOrigins.includes(origin)) {
      callback(null, true);
    } else {
      callback(new Error('Not allowed by CORS'));
    }
  },
  credentials: true,
}));

const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');

// General API traffic: generous, since most routes aren't individually sensitive
const generalLimiter = rateLimit({ windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000, max: 300 });

// Login: much stricter, since this route is a realistic brute-force target
const loginLimiter = rateLimit({ windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000, max: 5 });

// An endpoint that calls a paid, metered third-party SMS API per request:
// stricter still, since each request has a dollar cost attached
const smsLimiter = rateLimit({ windowMs: 60 * 60 * 1000, max: 10 });

app.use('/api/', generalLimiter);
app.post('/login', loginLimiter, loginHandler);
app.post('/send-sms', smsLimiter, sendSmsHandler);

